::ENG4_U01_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. What is the passage mainly about? {
~A. How transportation alone caused higher grades#The passage rejects this claim.
=B. How dependable transit can expand access to academic support services#Correct. This is the central conclusion.
~C. Why tutoring should replace college classes#The passage does not say this.
~D. Why libraries should stop offering workshops#The passage does not say this.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Transportation alone raised students' grades.#This overstates the source.
~B. The report ignored academic support services.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. The report claimed transportation had no academic value.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. Dependable transit was connected to increased access to academic support.#Correct. This preserves the source's conclusion.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. What does relevant mean in this lesson context? {
=A. Directly connected to a claim#Correct. Relevant evidence directly supports the point.
~B. Emotionally dramatic#Drama is not relevance.
~C. Longer than other evidence#Length does not determine relevance.
~D. Written in first person#Point of view does not determine relevance.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which claim does this evidence best support? {
~A. The college should end tutoring sessions.#The evidence supports access to tutoring, not ending it.
~B. Childcare was the only reason students missed workshops.#The evidence is about transportation and attendance.
=C. The shuttle route may have improved access to academic workshops.#Correct. The attendance evidence connects the shuttle to workshop participation.
~D. Printed books are no longer useful.#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which oral explanation has the clearest organization? {
~A. Transportation is good and students need help and the report said things.#This is vague and disorganized.
~B. The report is about college, so the shuttle is obviously best.#This lacks evidence and reasoning.
=C. Reliable transportation matters because the review found students with evening transit were more likely to attend support services after work.#Correct. This gives claim, evidence, and explanation.
~D. Students should attend everything because workshops exist.#This does not paraphrase or explain the source accurately.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The review recommended continuing the shuttle program while also studying other barriers, including childcare, course scheduling, and internet access. Which paraphrase is most complete? {
=A. The review advised keeping the shuttle while examining other obstacles such as childcare, schedules, and internet access.#Correct. This preserves all parts of the recommendation.
~B. The review advised ending the shuttle because other barriers exist.#This reverses the recommendation.
~C. The review said transportation was the only barrier students faced.#This ignores the listed barriers.
~D. The review focused only on internet access.#This is too narrow.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which phrase best defines evidence? {
~A. A personal preference about a topic#Preference is not evidence.
~B. A sentence that sounds formal#Formality alone is not evidence.
~C. A conclusion with no support#A conclusion needs evidence.
=D. Information from a source that supports or clarifies a claim#Correct. Evidence supports a claim.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. Which student statement distorts the passage? {
~A. The report links dependable transit with access to support services.#This is accurate.
=B. The report proves that transit alone raises grades.#Correct. This distorts the passage.
~C. The report avoids claiming transportation alone caused higher grades.#This is accurate.
~D. The report focuses on access to tutoring, workshops, and study groups.#This is accurate.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which sentence would work best in a brief oral explanation? {
~A. The shuttle is pretty much helpful because support is good.#This is vague.
~B. I think buses are better than workshops.#This is opinion without source support.
=C. Attendance records are relevant because they show workshop participation rose after the shuttle route was added.#Correct. This explains evidence relevance clearly.
~D. Academic services are everywhere all the time.#This is unsupported and vague.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which detail is most relevant to a claim about transportation and academic support access? {
=A. Students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend support services after work.#Correct. This directly supports the claim.
~B. The review was conducted by a community college.#This may give context but does not directly support the claim.
~C. The word transportation has many syllables.#This is irrelevant.
~D. Some students had jobs after work.#This alone does not show access to support.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The review recommended continuing the shuttle program while also studying other barriers, including childcare, course scheduling, and internet access. What does barriers mean in this context? {
~A. Decorations near a bus route#This is unrelated.
~B. Arguments that prove a conclusion#That is not the meaning here.
~C. Workshop attendance records#Records are evidence, not barriers.
=D. Obstacles that may limit student access#Correct. Barriers are obstacles.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which paraphrase is too vague? {
~A. Attendance at late-afternoon workshops increased after the shuttle route was added.#This is specific and accurate.
=B. Some stuff happened after the college changed transportation.#Correct. This is too vague to preserve the evidence.
~C. The records showed more students attended workshops after the shuttle connected to bus stops.#This is accurate.
~D. Workshop participation rose after the new shuttle route began.#This is accurate.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which response best uses logical organization? {
~A. Evidence, claim, maybe, shuttle, students, yes.#This is not organized.
=B. Claim: Transit matters. Evidence: Students with reliable transit attended support services more often. Explanation: This shows access can affect participation.#Correct. This uses claim, evidence, and explanation.
~C. The report is long, so the shuttle is useful.#Length does not prove usefulness.
~D. I would ride the shuttle because workshops are important.#This shifts to personal opinion.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. Why is the word alone important? {
~A. It proves transportation had no effect.#The passage does not say transportation had no effect.
~B. It means tutoring caused lower grades.#The passage does not say this.
=C. It limits the claim and prevents an exaggerated paraphrase.#Correct. The word limits the causal claim.
~D. It shows the report focused only on grades.#The passage focuses on access to support services.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which word best replaces increased without changing meaning? {
=A. Rose#Correct. Rose means increased.
~B. Declined#This is the opposite.
~C. Vanished#This means disappeared.
~D. Ignored#This means paid no attention to.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which paraphrase preserves the key idea? {
~A. Students with evening transportation avoided academic support.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Students with cars always earned higher grades.#This adds unsupported ideas.
~C. Students attended fewer workshops after work.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. Students with reliable evening transit had greater participation in after-work academic support options.#Correct. This preserves the key idea.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which phrase would be most effective in an oral explanation? {
~A. This evidence is cool because buses are good.#This is informal and vague.
~B. The passage is about transit, and transit is transit.#This is circular.
=C. This attendance evidence is relevant because it connects the shuttle route to increased workshop participation.#Correct. It explains relevance clearly.
~D. Attendance records exist in the report.#This does not explain relevance.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The review recommended continuing the shuttle program while also studying other barriers, including childcare, course scheduling, and internet access. Which claim is most accurate? {
=A. The review supported the shuttle but recognized that other barriers also needed study.#Correct. This preserves both ideas.
~B. The review recommended studying only transportation.#This omits other barriers.
~C. The review rejected the shuttle because of childcare.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. The review concluded internet access was irrelevant.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. Which academic word best describes the report's careful limitation? {
~A. Exaggeration#The report avoids exaggeration.
=B. Qualification#Correct. The report limits the claim.
~C. Decoration#This is unrelated.
~D. Confusion#The passage is clear, not confused.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which paraphrase copies too much of the original? {
~A. Workshop attendance rose after the shuttle route began.#This is concise and restated.
~B. Records showed more students joined late-day workshops once the shuttle connected to bus stops.#This is restated.
~C. The attendance data linked the new shuttle route with greater workshop participation.#This is restated.
=D. The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route.#Correct. This closely copies original wording and structure.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which choice best shows coherent focus in an oral explanation? {
=A. Focus on how reliable transportation affected access to academic support services.#Correct. This matches the passage and keeps the explanation focused.
~B. Talk about shuttles, then grades, then internet, then personal preference.#This lacks focus.
~C. Focus on whether libraries should buy more printed books.#This is unrelated.
~D. Focus only on whether buses are comfortable.#This is not the passage's issue.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. What is the best evidence-based explanation? {
~A. The report says transit caused higher grades without other factors.#This contradicts the limitation.
~B. The report says support services were unrelated to transit.#This contradicts the conclusion.
=C. The report connects transit with access to support services, but it avoids claiming transit alone improved grades.#Correct. This preserves both the conclusion and the limitation.
~D. The report says students should stop using support services.#This is unsupported.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. What does participation mean? {
~A. Refusal to attend#This is the opposite.
~B. A written recommendation#That is not participation.
~C. A transportation barrier#That is not participation.
=D. Attendance or involvement#Correct. Participation means taking part.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The review recommended continuing the shuttle program while also studying other barriers, including childcare, course scheduling, and internet access. Which student paraphrase is misleading? {
~A. The review recommended keeping the shuttle program.#This is accurate but partial.
=B. The review concluded that no other barriers mattered once the shuttle existed.#Correct. This contradicts the passage.
~C. The review said childcare, scheduling, and internet access should also be studied.#This is accurate.
~D. The review suggested that transportation was one part of a larger access issue.#This is accurate.
}

::ENG4_U01_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which explanation best combines claim, evidence, and relevance? {
=A. The shuttle likely improved access to support services because attendance records showed workshop participation rose after the route was added.#Correct. This combines claim, evidence, and relevance.
~B. The shuttle was added near bus stops, and many colleges have buses.#This does not explain relevance.
~C. Workshop participation is a phrase from the passage.#This identifies wording but not reasoning.
~D. The evidence is relevant because it is evidence.#This is circular and weak.
}
